3 What are the lakes of the Great Rift Valley?
As we said at the beginning, the Rift Valley began to form about 30 million years ago as a result of the separation of the tectonic plates (Somali, Indian, Arabian and Eurasian). As time passes and the earth's crust is melted by the molten magma that rises to the surface, a long trench is formed with slopes that have a great slope.
The Rift Valley, located to the east of the African continent, has an extension of 4830 kilometers. In its eastern part we find the typical African savannas, where the African buffalo, the wildebeest, the giraffe or the lion live; and in the west it hosts jungles, which are the habitat of chimpanzees and gorillas, among others.
As a consequence of the separation exerted by the Rift Valley, in the east of the continent the climate is drier than in the west, which is why in this part of Africa the savannah appeared first, and then the local apes that until then lived in the trees. Some time later they must have become terrestrial, learning to walk on their two hind legs that we know today as legs.
The lakes that are in this valley are some of the richest in biodiversity in the world. Until now 800 species of cichlid fish have been discovered (bony fish), and there are still many more that are still waiting to be discovered.
Their names are:
It is a layer denser than the interior of the earth and less dense as it approaches the surface. It is also called the mesosphere.
It is the set of the entire area of water that exists in the earth's crust. You can find all the seas and oceans, lakes and rivers, groundwater and glaciers.
The atmosphere in turn is divided into different layers, each with a different length, function and composition.
Thermosphere it is called this way because, due to the effect of solar radiation, temperatures can exceed 1500 ° C. In it is an area called the ionosphere, in which many atoms lose electrons and are in the form of ions, releasing energy that constitutes the northern lights.
The biosphere it is not a layer of the Earth itself, but it is the set of all the ecosystems that exist. All living beings that inhabit our planet make up the biosphere.
