The tropopause has differential characteristics between the layers it separates and is what makes the climate end up line. In this post we tell you all about the tropopause.
The openings in the tropopause allow the ozone present in the stratosphere and the rest of the dry air to enter the troposphere. The altitude values of the tropopause descend in the areas from the equator to the poles.
In this way, three different conditions or three types of tropopause can be identified, depending on the area where it is as well as the latitude and altitude.
It is the one that signals when there is a type of warm advection in the upper and middle zone of the troposphere. This usually happens in the presence of warm anticyclones.
Serves as a water reservoir, since it is capable of storing a lot of water vapor in its lower limit from tropical areas. Many of the compounds present in this limit serve to better understand the effects of climate change and how it will affect the planet.
The first is that, as CO2 concentrations increase, they increase the number of collisions that molecules have with other gases such as nitrogen. During these shocks there is an absorption of kinetic energy and it is when what is known as infrared radiation is produced.
The second phenomenon that occurs due to the tropopause is that It occurs with increasing concentrations of CO2. In this case, it absorbs the heat that comes from the ground and there is an increase in temperature in the lower part of the atmosphere.
In geology there have been great scientists who have performed incredible feats, thus helping to advance and learn more about our planet. Among the most prominent scientists we have Charles lyell.
4 Honors and travels of Charles Lyell
In these years there was another more widespread theory about the formation of the Earth that was catastrophism. This idea defended that the Earth had been built and shaped by a series of great catastrophes that had happened in a very short time, but that had transformed all the terrestrial dynamics and the relief.
Thanks to these postulates, the work of Charles Lyell was very successful throughout the world. It served as an inspiration to many scientists, including Charles Darwin.
Lyell was considered one of the founders of stratigraphy, in which they could begin to study the different layers of the Earth's surface. He was one of the first authors to classify the strata through some studies of marine strata carried out in Western Europe.
He was a member of the Royal Society, in which he could better develop his studies. Although James Hutton had previously published that the Earth had been formed from slow actions and not sudden changes as defended by catastrophism, it was Lyell who gave the most clarifying explanations at that time.
I hope this information has helped you meet one of the founding fathers of modern geology.
Surely you have heard many times in recent years about climate change and the consequences it can have in the medium and long term. But, Do you really know what the term itself means and if it is as serious as they say?
4.1 Effects on living beings
Main causes The causes of climate change can be natural o anthropogenic, that is, by human action.
All of them have at some time caused a major climate change. For example, 65 million years ago an asteroid hit the Earth and ended up causing an Ice Age, wiping out the few dinosaurs that were left alive after the disaster.
It had not been possible to speak that man could aggravate climate change until el Homo sapiens will start to deforest the forests to turn them into farmland. It is true that during that time (about 10 years ago) the human race did not exceed five million, that although it is an important figure, the impact on the Earth was much less than today.
When talking about this process, reference is made to the retention of heat from the sun in the atmosphere by a layer of gases (such as CO2, methane, or nitrous oxide) found in it. It is important to know that without this effect there could not be life as we know it, because the planet would simply be too cold.
We know the temperatures are going to rise, but What do we have to face?
Plants and animals will also be affected: spring events such as flowering or egg laying will come early. Some species will stop migrating, and others will instead be forced to do so if they want to survive.
Moreover, the drought will intensify in those regions where rainfall is scarce in itself.
Climate changes have been and always will be. Currently, however, humans are doing too much to speed it up and make it worse.
