Surely you have ever heard about the phenomenon of evapotranspiration when talking about plants. In effect, it is a phenomenon that occurs when plants lose water from their tissues due to two phenomena that act as a whole: evaporation on the one hand and perspiration on the other.
4 Importance in agriculture
Since these two phenomena are difficult to measure separately, they occur together as evapotranspiration. In most of the cases this is studied, you need to know the total amount of water that is lost to the atmosphere and the process by which it is lost does not matter.
Evapotranspiration is an important variable in the field of agronomic sciences. It is considered an important element considering the water needs that crops have so that they can develop correctly.
On the other hand we have the actual evapotranspiration (ETR). In this case, we measure the actual amount of water that evapotranspires based on the existing conditions in each case.
It means losing water resources that cannot be used. We must also bear in mind that it is one more element of the hydrological cycle of water and that, sooner or later, everything that has evaporated will one day precipitate again.
