Because of the 6 thousand stars that can be seen with the naked eye from Earth, as long as it is a dark night without light pollution, one stands out: Polaris or Polaris. And not because it is the brightest, the largest or the closest to us, but because, unlike the rest, it always remains at the same point in the sky.
The explanation is that this star, located in the constellation Ursa Minor, is in a very special place, right on the axis of rotation of the Earth. To give us an idea, if space were a disk spinning on a record player, Polaris would be right in the center, always immobile while the rest of the stars move around us.
4 And what about the Southern Hemisphere?
