When it comes to unexplained mysteries, very few seem to be as incredible and irrefutable as the Ural Relief Map. In 1995, Aleksander Chuvyrov, Professor of Mathematical and Physical Science at Bashkir State University in Russia, was investigating hypotheses of the immigration of Chinese Migrants to Siberia and the Urals.
As they were supposedly situated in an area central to his study, a remote village named Chandar in the southern Urals, Chuvyrov thought the stones could be of Chinese Origin. He organized a team and helicopter to try and locate them.
The tablet (Dashka Stone) appears to show a highly accurate topographical map of Bashkiria, a specific area of the Ural Mountains, at a scale of approximately 1:1.1km
A geological test of the slab concluded that it consisted of three layers, the base being 14cm thick dolomite, the second being a diopside glass which was totally unknown to science till then, while the third is a protective layer of calcium porcelain. Chuvyrov said, “It should be noted, that the relief has not been manually made by an ancient stonecutter.
Soil tests of 400 types of soil in the area, in comparison to the ones found embedded on the stone, have allowed scientists to narrow down the possible location of other pieces to four specific areas around the village of Chandar. If the Map of the Creator is genuine then it would suggest the existence of an ancient highly developed civilization.
