The skeletons were reported to be at or above 7 feet (2.1 meters) in height, possessing skulls that had horn-like protuberances just above the eyebrows. © Image Credit: Public Domain
Neither Donehoo, Skinner, nor Moorehead described the discovery of any human skeletons at Sayre exhibiting gigantism or horned protrusions in their official excavation reports. And articles about this strange discovery appeared in journals and magazines and then somehow they disappeared and were never seen again, taking with them the riddle that forever belongs to the unsolved.
The solar storm that occurred 2,700 years ago was documented in Assyrian tablets
Things that are eerie or mysterious have a strange influence on us, and they frequently drive our thoughts insane with the desire to find a convincing explanation. When the family of Charles E. Peck got multiple phone calls for 12 hours after he died in a terrific railway accident, it confused even the skeptics and provided hope to those who believe in life after death.
Unfortunately, the couple would never be able to go down the aisle. And the manner of Peck’s death in one of the deadliest train accidents in US history would create a mystery that has yet to be solved.
Getting a job there would have allowed him to marry his fiancée Andrea from Westlake Village. Although the couple was ready to get married, the fact that they did not live in the same state posed a problem.
On September 12, 2008, Charles boarded a plane to Los Angeles for the interview and then caught the Metrolink to its last stop at Moorpark, where Andrea had arranged to pick him up. There were 225 people on that Friday evening train and it was due to arrive at its final destination at 4.45 p.m.
Andrea, Peck’s fiancée, was heading to the railway station to pick him up when she heard about the collision on the radio. Spurred on by the assumption that he was still alive, Andrea screamed her encouragement to Peck that assistance was on the way every time a connection got through and she heard silence on the other end of the line.
Throughout the night, firemen and police worked to collect the other victims from the carriages, using the signal from Pecks’ phone to attempt to locate him. The calls finally came to a halt about 3:00 a.m. the next morning.
To his family’s dismay, the guy they adored had died. However, when doctors examined his corpse, they realized he could not have survived the initial collision.
Another popular hypothesis is that the device malfunctioned, which is a possibility. However, it does not explain why the calls appeared to be limited to his nearest and dearest and were not reported to his wider contacts.
Is it possible that Peck somehow crossed the barrier between this world and the next to lead his family to his body and bid them farewell?
How or why the phone calls continued for so long and so frequently after his death is a mystery that will most likely never be solved.
Mystery Nan Madol: A mysterious hi-tech city built 14,000 years ago?
The mysterious city of Nan Madol lies in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, more than 1,000 km from the nearest coast. It’s a metropolis built in the middle of nowhere, for which it is also known as the “Venice of the Pacific.”
Micronesia is an independent country of the United States, consisting of the Yap, Chuuk, Pohnpei, and Kosrae regions along the western edge of the Pacific Ocean. The four regions of Micronesia consist of a total of 707 islands.
In some places, the basalt rock wall that has been built across the island in the middle of the Pacific Ocean is 25 feet high and 18 feet thick. Signs of human habitation are found all over the island city, but experts have not yet been able to determine which modern human ancestors lived in the city.
Therefore, Nan Madol must have been higher than the sea in the period when it was built. But according to geologists, the island on which Nan Madol is located never sank due to phenomena such as bradyseism, like other cities that are now below sea level, for example, ancient Siponto in Italy.
For mainstream researchers, this is unacceptable, which is why you read on Wikipedia that Nan Madol was built in the 2nd century AD by the Saudeleurs. But that is only the date of the oldest human remains found on the island, not of its actual construction.
In fact, Nan Madol is not built on land, but in the sea, like Venice.
Another enigmatic part of the ancient city is that the rock of which Nan Madol is made is ‘magnetic rock’. If one brings a compass close to the rock, it goes crazy.
The city of Nan Madol thrived until AD 1628, when Isokelekel, a semi-mythical hero warrior from the island of Kosrae conquered the Saudeleur Dynasty and established the Nahnmwarki Era. © Image Credit: Ajdemma | Flickr
The term was introduced by Augustus Le Plongeon, who used the “Land of Mu” as an alternative name for Atlantis. It was subsequently popularized as an alternative term for the hypothetical land of Lemuria by James Churchward, who asserted that Mu was located in the Pacific Ocean before its destruction.
The 1999 bookThe Coming Global Superstorm by Art Bell and Whitley Strieber, which predicts that global warming might produce sudden and catastrophic climatic effects, claims that the construction of Nan Madol, with exacting tolerances and extremely heavy basalt materials, necessitated a high degree of technical competency. Since no such society exists in the modern record this society must have been destroyed by dramatic means.
Although the last Ice Age ended 14,000 years ago, the seas did not finally stop rising until about 6,500 years ago which is why there are submerged villages and towns etc all around the Mediterranean. Still, its an impressive feat to construct this city.
