1 What is the sun
3 What is the sun: internal structure What is the sun
Scientists claim that the basis of the sun is all the nuclear reactions that occur in a reactor. We could consider the common sun a fairly typical star even though it has a mass, radius, and other properties that are outside of what is considered the average for stars.
Unlike other stars in the universe, the sun is almost completely round in shape. If we look from our planet, we can see a perfectly circular disk.
The angular size of the sun is about half a degree if the measurement is taken from planet Earth.
The power of the sun is equal to more than ten raised to 23 kilowatts. In contrast, the radiant power of known incandescent bulbs is less than 0,1 kilowatts.
To facilitate the study and understanding of the characteristics of the sun, its structure is divided into 6 layers. It is distributed in very different areas and starts from the inside.
The temperature here is not as high as the core of the earth, but it has reached around 5 million Kelvin. The energy is converted into photons, which are transmitted and reabsorbed many times by the particles that make up the plasma.
Photosphere: It is part of the apparent surface of the star and we always wanted it. The sun is not entirely solid, but is made of plasma.
Visible to the naked eye, its temperature is about 2 million Kelvin. It is not clear why the temperature of this layer is so high, but they are related to the strong magnetic field generated by the sun.
