2 Study on the collapse of the Gulf Stream
An atmospheric physicist from the Complutense University of Madrid said: “Once this happens, the movement of warm tropical waters towards the North Atlantic region will cease, they will become cooler waters and will have an impact on the region's climate. According to the study, published in the journal Nature, the behavior of this water "conveyor belt" - known as the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation -AMOC- has given enough signs that it is on the verge of an "imminent collapse".
This is in line with the increased density of surface waters caused by deepening climate change, bringing the system one step closer to total collapse.
The study did not specify when the phenomenon could occur, but it does not rule out that it will happen in the coming decades, possibly even before the end of the century. "This would have catastrophic effects on Europe and the world as a whole," as it would suddenly "completely alter the climate," the researchers concluded.
"This situation may be worse than warming, as its effects are felt gradually, but nevertheless this is a fundamental change that will still have unintended effects," he insists. Potential impacts include reduced rainfall, denser snow cover in more parts of Europe, agricultural problems or a higher probability of events such as stronger cyclones.
These include freshwater inflows from melting Greenland ice sheets, melting sea ice, increased precipitation and river water.
The Atlantic Meridional Circulation is an important ocean current that regulates Earth's climate because it carries warm water from the surface at high latitudes, warms the air, sinks, and returns to the equator. For example, it is responsible for Spain, which enjoys a much milder climate compared to the rest of the planet at our same latitude.
