20 Highest Paying States for Cybersecurity Jobs in 2022
Before we get into the highest paying states for cybersecurity jobs in 2022, it’s important to remember that the demand for cybersecurity experts is increasing and doesn’t appear to be slowing down anytime soon. Although the talent gap for certified cybersecurity experts is widening, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics predicts a 31% increase in employment for cybersecurity analysts between 2019 and 2029.
That being said, anyone looking to advance their careers as a cybersecurity professional should be aware of key industry numbers and statistics, such as the highest paying states for cybersecurity jobs based on factors such as high average salaries, long-term growth potential, and high employment per 1,000 jobs.
According to a recent Comparitech study, 9 states will be the best cities for cybersecurity jobs in 2022 .
The state has the most cybersecurity professionals employed, the highest employment per 1,000 jobs, a high annual government cyber security jobs salary ($114,760), and a strong long-term growth projection for cybersecurity roles (by 2028). Northern Virginia’s role in federal contracting is critical to the state’s position at the top of the pyramid.
The state also has 6 companies listed in the Cyber 150, the 4th most of any state in the country. Virginia is an ideal hub for cybersecurity companies and professionals due to its proximity to the Pentagon, the CIA, the Navy Cyber Defense Operations Command, the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency (DARPA), and other federal assets.
According to the most recent Business Facilities rankings, Texas is the leading state in terms of cybersecurity growth potential, outperforming Virginia, Louisiana, Maryland, and Georgia. The metrics used to determine the rankings included higher education resources, employment rates, research labs, and innovation centers, and the overall cyber ecosystem.
It’s also worth noting that San Antonio has emerged as a leading cybersecurity hub, with the highest concentration of cyber and intelligence experts outside of the national capital region. Texas currently employs 10,430 cybersecurity analysts and has approximately 3,400 cybersecurity job openings.
New York City According to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, New York ranks 4th among states with the highest employment levels and 1st among the highest paying states for cybersecurity jobs .
According to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, the District of Columbia has the 2nd highest concentration of cybersecurity jobs and the 3rd highest location quotient in the country. Aside from employing over 2,000 cyber experts, the state has an employment per thousand jobs ratio of 2.80, which is relatively high when compared to many states, which have an employment level of less than 2.0.
Colorado also has one of the highest location quotients, which is a measure of the concentrations of a state’s cybersecurity demand relative to the national average, according to CyberSeek. The state ranks 4th with a location quotient of 2.40, trailing only D.C. (7.9), Virginia (4.6), and Maryland (2.7).
The Cybersecurity Growth Potential rankings are based on an overall assessment of each state’s cyber ecosystem, which includes higher education resources, collaborations with national security programs, research labs, and innovation centers, and employment statistics.
Maryland employs more than 5,860 cybersecurity experts, according to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Its employment per thousand jobs ratio of 2.17 and location quotient of 2.54 makes it one of the highest paying states for cybersecurity jobs.
New York Finance firms in New York employ thousands of cybersecurity professionals, making it one of the best cities for cybersecurity jobs in 2022 .
New York Works is a 10-year plan focused on future jobs, with $30 million set aside to assist in the creation of up to 10,000 cybersecurity jobs supporting office districts in Manhattan and surrounding boroughs. The average government cybersecurity jobs salary in New York is well over $100,000, making it one of the best cities for cybersecurity jobs in 2022.
It’s no surprise that thousands of cybersecurity jobs are available in the western financial and technology capital. Several large technology companies, as well as government headquarters, Health Care Providers, hospitals, and school systems, are among the top employers.
Chicago’s prominence in cybersecurity is unsurprising given its status as one of the largest business hubs in the United States. When we checked, there were over 4,000 related cybersecurity job openings in Chicago.
Arlington is located directly across the Potomac River from Washington, D.C., and is home to the Pentagon, the Department of Defense, and one of the world’s largest employers of cybersecurity professionals (often through private contractors). Arlington has a high demand for cybersecurity professionals, with close to the highest number of openings in a variety of job titles, including less common ones like encryption expert, incident responder, and penetration testers.
Atlanta has the nation’s 10th largest economy and the 4th most Fortune 500 companies, thereby making it one of the best cities for cybersecurity jobs in 2022. In addition, 75% of Fortune 500 companies have a presence in the city, often in the form of regional headquarters.
Security administrators and security engineers are the most common job titles for cybersecurity roles in Anne Arundel County. Annapolis Junction and Fort Meade, Maryland have particularly high concentrations of cybersecurity professionals.
It’s no secret that organizations of all sizes are ramping up their hiring efforts now that the cybercrime epidemic is everyone’s concern. As a result, information technology professionals and others who want to position themselves for success in this critical and rapidly growing field are honing their skills through industry certifications and advanced degree programs.
As a cybersecurity professional, you aid in the protection of sensitive data and information both online and on the servers of your employer. You manage firewalls, encryption, and databases, always trying to stay ahead of any security threats that might jeopardize your employer’s security.
According to the BLS, job growth in this field will be 28% between 2016 and 2026. This is a significantly faster rate of growth than the national average for all occupations.
An information technology professional working in cybersecurity, or cybersecurity, has more tools than ever before; however, hackers do as well. Cybersecurity is a career in which security professionals strive to always be one step ahead of threats to their employer, and when a breach occurs, they seek to rectify the situation as soon as possible and ensure it never happens again.
A bachelor’s degree in cybersecurity, computer science, or a related field will prepare students for the majority of entry-level cybersecurity jobs.
Prospective cybersecurity experts should pursue studies in areas such as cybersecurity, computer science, or a related field. Most cybersecurity employers require at least a bachelor’s degree, but some prefer applicants with a master’s degree.
A bachelor’s degree in cybersecurity provides students with the knowledge and practical skills required for a wide range of well-paying jobs. According to PayScale, those with a BS in cybersecurity earn an average annual salary of $69,773.
cybersecurity analysts, computer network analysts, security software developers, and security specialists are among the top entry-level cybersecurity jobs.
